the knot of a band, Gr. πεῖσμα (for *πενθμα, *φενθμα), a rope. See ''band''<sup>1</sup>, ''band''<sup>2</sup>, ''bend''<sup>1</sup>, ''bend''<sup>2</sup>, etc., ''bond''<sup>1</sup>, ''bundle'', etc.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To make fast (to, on, or upon) with a band or bond of any kind.
{{smaller block|Thou shalt ''bind'' them for a sign ''upon'' thine hand.
{{right|Deut. vi. 8.}}
''Bind'' the chariot ''to'' the swift beast. Micah i. 13.}}
'''2.''' To unite by any legal or moral tie; attach by considerations of love, duty, interest, obligation, etc.: as, ''bound'' in the bonds of matrimony; ''bound'' by gratitude, duty, debt, etc.
{{smaller block|<poem>Distrust and grief
Will ''bind'' to us each Western chief.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', L. of the L., ii. 30.}}}}
'''3.''' To put in bonds or fetters; deprive of liberty or of the use of the limbs by making fast physically.
{{smaller block|''Bind'' him hand and foot, and take him away.
{{right|Mat. xxii. 13.}}
He took Paul’s girdle, and ''bound'' his own hands and feet, and said, . . . So shall the Jews at Jerusalem ''bind'' the man that owneth this girdle. Acts xxi. 11.}}
'''4.''' To restrain; hold to a particular state, place, employment, etc.
{{smaller block|He ''bindeth'' the floods from overflowing. Job xxviii. 11.
I have no official business to ''bind'' me.
{{right|''Macaulay'', in Trevelyan, II. vii.}}}}
'''5.''' To hinder or restrain (the bowels) from their natural operations; make costive; constipate.—'''6.''' To fasten around anything; fix in place by girding or tying: as, to ''bind'' a cord round the arm.
{{smaller block|I, maiden, round thee, maiden, ''bind'' my belt.}}

readings.c2s2:
{{smaller block|{{right|''Tennyson'', Holy Grail.}}}}

¶
'''7.''' To encircle with a band or ligature; gird; confine or restrain by girding: as, “''bind'' up those tresses,” ''Shak.'', K. John, iii. 4.
{{smaller block|A fillet ''binds'' her hair. {{right|''Pope'', Windsor Forest, l. 178.}}}}

¶
'''8.''' To swathe or bandage; cover and swathe with dressings: with ''up''.
{{smaller block|He healeth the broken in heart, and ''bindeth up'' their wounds. {{right|Ps. cxlvii. 3.}}

Give me another horse, ''bind up'' my wounds.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich III., v. 3.}}}}

¶
'''9.''' To form a border or edge on, for the purpose of strengthening or ornamenting; edge: as, to ''bind'' a wheel with a tire; to ''bind'' a garment or a carpet.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her mantle rich, whose borders round
A deep and fretted broidery ''bound''.</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, vi. 3.}}
<poem>Black cliffs and high,
With green grass growing on the tops of them,
''Binding'' them round as gold a garment’s hem.</poem>
{{right|''William Morris'', Earthly Paradise, I. 172.}}}}

¶
'''10.''' To tie or fasten (loose things) together with a band, cord, or tie; tie up into one bundle or mass: as, to ''bind'' sheaves of grain.—'''11.''' To fasten or secure within a cover, as a book or pamphlet. See ''bookbinding''.—'''12.''' In ''fencing'', to secure (the sword of an adversary). See ''binding'', ''n.'', 3.—'''13.''' To cause to cohere; cement; knit; unite firmly: as, to ''bind'' the loose sand.
{{smaller block|<poem>The sooner to effect,
And surer ''bind'', this knot of amity,—</poem>}}

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|<poem>The Earl of Armagnac . . .
Proffers his only daughter to your grace
In marriage. {{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 1.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|God has so ''bound'' society together that if one member suffer, all suffer. {{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 60.}}}}
{{smaller block|Have enough oil in the colours to ''bind'' them.
{{right|''Workshop Receipts'', 1st ser., p. 423.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Binding'' the ink to prevent its smearing.
{{right|''Workshop Receipts'', 2d ser., p. 343.}}}}
'''14.''' To place under obligation or compulsion: as, all are ''bound'' to obey the laws.
{{smaller block|<poem>This ring I gave him, when he parted from me,
To ''bind'' him to remember my good-will.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. G. of V., iv. 4.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|’Tis true, by my father’s will, I am for a short period ''bound'' to regard you as his substitute.
{{right|''Sheridan'', School for Scandal, iii. 1.}}}}
'''15.''' To put under legal obligation: often with ''over'': as, to ''bind'' a man ''over'' to keep the peace. Specifically—'''16.''' To indenture as an apprentice: often with ''out''.
{{smaller block|My mother she wanted to ''bind'' me ''out'' to a blacksmith.
{{right|''Mrs. Stowe'', Oldtown, p. 83.}}}}

{{smaller|'''To bind hand and foot.''' See ''hand''.—'''To bind in,''' to inclose; surround.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Bound in'' with the triumphant sea.
{{right|''Shak.'', Rich. II., ii. 1.}}
A costly jewel . . . ''bound in'' with diamonds.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 2.}}</poem>}}

{{smaller|'''To bind up in,''' to cause to be wholly engrossed with; absorb in; connect intimately with: chiefly in the passive.}}
{{smaller block|Seeing that his life is ''bound up in'' the lad’s life.
{{right|Gen. xliv. 30.}}}}
